WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
SETH VARGO: In this episode
of the Google Secret Manager

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.010
video series, we'll
talk about how

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.650
Secret Manager can aid
in threat detection

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.810
and forensic analysis.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.150
Where can you find a
list of all requests

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.830
to Google Secret Manager?

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.538
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:15.538 --> 00:00:18.616
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.350
While we always want
to do as much as we

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.340
can to prevent a data breach,
a good security threat model

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.729
also accounts for the
unexpected and helps us respond

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.950
in the event of a compromise.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.630
All too often, organizations
invest all their resources

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.290
in prevention and then
they leave no bandwidth

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.810
for detection or recovery.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.320
Cloud Audit Logging is a
fully managed logging service

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.440
that, once enabled,
provides a forensic history

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.380
of interactions with
Google Secret Manager.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.260
It also works with other
Google Cloud products,

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:03.490
but this episode focuses
specifically on Secret Manager.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.010
To tell us more about
Cloud Audit Logging

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.320
and how it can help
with forensics,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.376
I think we need to
bring in an expert.

00:01:10.376 --> 00:01:13.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.350
Hey, Tommy.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:15.517
Thanks for joining us today.

00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:16.642
TOMMY MURPHY: Thanks, Seth.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:17.750
Thank you for having me.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.200
SETH VARGO: So Tommy is
a software engineer who

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.720
works on Google Secret Manager.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.340
Tommy, earlier, I was telling
our viewers about Cloud Audit

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.220
Logging and how it
keeps a detailed list

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.955
of all interactions with
Google Secret Manager.

00:01:32.955 --> 00:01:34.330
TOMMY MURPHY:
That's right, Seth.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.280
But there's an
important first step.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.450
SETH VARGO: Oh, what's that?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.075
TOMMY MURPHY: You
have to turn them on.

00:01:39.075 --> 00:01:42.640
Admin write audit logs, which
include any changes to secrets,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.220
are enabled by
default. But admin

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.700
read logs, which include
viewing secret metadata,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.360
and data read logs,
which log secret access,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.540
are disabled by default
because of the amount of data

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.380
they generate.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.005
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:01:57.005 --> 00:01:58.660
But what if I have
my secret stored

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.700
across multiple projects?

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.060
Do I have to enable it on each
project, or how does that work?

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.280
TOMMY MURPHY: You can
enable cloud audit logs

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.889
on a per-product basis.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:10.720
And you can choose
to enable logs

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.280
for admin read, admin write,
data read, data write,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.695
or any combination.

00:02:16.695 --> 00:02:17.320
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.350
But to rephrase
my last question,

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.560
where do I turn them on,
at the project level,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.090
or how do I manage
this at scale?

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.400
TOMMY MURPHY: Great question.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.000
Cloud audit logs are
configured through IAM

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.760
and can be enabled
on a single project,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.850
for all projects in a
folder, or for all projects

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.170
in an entire organization.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.710
This means a security
team can configure

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.830
an organization-wide
policy that automatically

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.950
applies to all current
and future projects.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:48.940
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.160
And then what do I do
after I turn them on?

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.090
TOMMY MURPHY: That's it.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:59.950
SETH VARGO: Oh, this is
kind of awkward then.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.710
TOMMY MURPHY: Why?

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:03.670
SETH VARGO: We just--

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.510
we had a lot more back-and-forth
with our other experts.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.370
I guess Cloud Audit Logging
really is this easy.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:11.120
TOMMY MURPHY: Yep.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.210
I'm going to go now.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.280
SETH VARGO: Cool.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.330
Thanks, Tommy.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.160
TOMMY MURPHY: Thanks, Seth.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.120
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.750
Let's look at how we enable
and consume cloud audit

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.700
logs for Google Secret Manager.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.250
Now that we've enabled
Cloud Audit Logging,

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.180
all requests to access and
manage secrets in this project

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.430
will generate a log entry.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.450
I'll go ahead and
access a secret

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.150
so it generates such
an audit log entry.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.290
These audit logs are written to
Cloud Logging, Google Cloud's

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.650
centralized logging solution,
which you can easily

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.560
access from the sidebar.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.470
From here, you can
filter by log type,

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:03.790
including audit logs
for particular services.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.940
For example, suppose
you suspected

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.950
that a service
account credential had

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.690
been compromised.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.710
Here in the Log Viewer, you
can filter for all requests

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:18.399
to access secrets using
that service account.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:22.600
You can then correlate that data
against timestamps, source IP

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.910
addresses, and more.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.010
Google Secret
Manager's integration

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.560
with Cloud Audit
Logging brings a wealth

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.740
of forensic information
to your fingertips

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.000
with the click of a button.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.790
That's all for this
episode, and this concludes

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.950
our Secret Manager series.

